{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794770632,"nonce":"TbBzN+ByXhAHIEztS8BSmlmklbk=","message":"Third nAIR slice, 5bafd7ea: methods resolved from the class hierarchy. A call of obj.m(...) is now one direct Call when no class below the receiver's class overrides m, even where HashLink would use the method table, and a CallMethod through the table otherwise, with every function the table may name requested and lowered (TypeBuilder::resolve_method; the receiver's class comes from its type, not from the node). Also super(...) and super.m(...) as direct calls on this, a subclass value where its superclass is expected (an UnsafeCast), and method tables in the reference interpreter. Checked on real Haxe (crates/ash_tast/tests/virtuals): a Square receiver calls Square.area directly, a Plain receiver calls the Shape.area it inherits, areaOf(Shape) and Shape.twice go through the table, and the interpreter dispatches to the right answers (24, 18). This is the TAST-only fact the bytecode lacks: the class set is closed. The assumption is stated in resolve_method's doc: a class added after the file is made (loaded, or hot-reloaded into a running program) adds an implementation this does not see, and the nAIR path is not connected to reload. Not done: call summaries for CallMethod (a method-table call is still a barrier; its summary could be the join of its targets' summaries, which are now all lowered), interface-typed receivers (virtuals), statics, strings, arrays, generics, and the nair2hl bridge on objects. Next: summaries for method-table calls, which lets the Shape.twice-style loop keep its loads.","files":null}]}